        <div id="victory" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. What Happened to the Aviron Victory Treadmill?</h2>
<p>
            Aviron's first treadmill was announced in October 2024 at $2,499, with a 22-inch
            touchscreen and a deck about 4 inches off the floor, and coverage at the time said
            deliveries would start in early 2025. It was sold as the Victory Treadmill. Garage Gym
            Reviews gives it 12.5 mph, 12% incline and a 400 lb user limit, and its Amazon listing
            agrees on the speed and incline and adds a 3.5 CHP motor. In October 2025 Aviron added the Victory Treadmill Plus, which kept the same
            footprint and moved to a 27-inch screen.
          </p>
          <p>
            When we checked on 20 October 2026, both Victory product pages on avironactive.com
            redirected to the treadmill shop page, which lists only the Justice, Podium and Summit,
            and the Victory was missing from the price list Aviron publishes for its current range.
            Aviron has not announced a discontinuation that we could find, but in practice the
            Victory is no longer sold on Aviron's US store. Amazon listings for the Victory and
            Victory Plus still appeared in search results; we have not confirmed their current
            price, stock or seller.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">If you are offered a Victory</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>It has a genuine advantage.</strong> The 4-inch step-up and 400 lb limit suit some buyers better than any of the new models, which have lower user limits and whose step-up height Aviron does not publish.</li>
            <li><strong>Check where it comes from.</strong> Aviron's 20-year warranty requires a purchase made directly through its website, and its warranty page excludes units bought from unauthorised sellers. Ask the seller what warranty applies, and in whose name.</li>
            <li><strong>Used units carry no warranty.</strong> Aviron's terms say neither its standard nor its 20-year warranty transfers to a new owner.</li>
            <li><strong>Price it against the Justice.</strong> At Aviron's current prices, a new Justice with a full warranty and a 30-day trial costs $1,999. A Victory needs to cost clearly less to make sense.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

