        <div id="membership" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Aviron Membership: What It Adds and What Works Without It</h2>
<p>
            Aviron calls its membership "recommended but not required", and on this point it is
            more generous than most connected-fitness brands. The prices on Aviron's membership page
            on 20 October 2026, which Garage Gym Reviews' reviews also give, were:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Annual:</strong> $29 a month, billed as $348 once a year.</li>
            <li><strong>Monthly:</strong> $34 a month with no term; monthly plans can be paused twice a year.</li>
            <li><strong>Perpetual:</strong> $2,499 once, with no renewals. Note that this does <em>not</em> keep the 20-year warranty active (see the next section).</li>
            <li><strong>Extra Aviron machines:</strong> $10 a month on a monthly or annual plan, or $999 once on a perpetual plan. One membership covers unlimited household profiles.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">With the membership</h3>
          <p>
            The membership unlocks the reason most people buy an Aviron: the games, live
            competition, coached classes, Les Mills workouts, guided programmes, scenic
            destinations, leaderboards, teams, monthly challenges and levels. Aviron's games are
            built so that your speed and incline drive what happens on screen, and Garage Gym
            Reviews' Podium reviewer describes them as genuinely different from watching something
            while you run. Some workouts change the incline for you, according to the same review.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Without the membership</h3>
          <p>
            Aviron says the machine works fully as a treadmill the moment it is assembled. Without a
            membership you keep the metrics screen (which Aviron says runs without an internet
            connection), manual speed and incline control, unlimited profiles, workout history,
            badges, friends, the MyAviron companion app and — unusually — the streaming apps,
            including Netflix, Prime Video, Disney+, YouTube and Spotify. Aviron also says workouts
            sync to Strava and Apple Health. That is considerably more than Peloton's Just Run mode,
            which our <a href="/peloton-tread/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Peloton Tread guide</a>
            describes, or Echelon's free tier, covered in our
            <a href="/echelon-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Echelon treadmill guide</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            The general caution applies all the same: what a connected treadmill does without a
            subscription is decided by software, and software can change. Buy on the basis of what
            the machine does today, and keep a copy of the terms.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Can you use Aviron on another treadmill?</h3>
          <p>
            Not in any way we could confirm. Aviron's standalone app, launched for iOS in March 2024,
            was built for rowers and paired with Concept2's RowErg. We found no Aviron page that
            lists a third-party treadmill its app supports. Some treadmill makers name Aviron among
            compatible apps — TKO does for its manual curved trainer, as our
            <a href="/tko-airraid-runner-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">TKO AirRaid Runner review</a>
            notes — but until Aviron itself confirms the pairing, do not buy another treadmill
            expecting to run Aviron's games on it. If open-app compatibility is what matters, our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-zwift/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Zwift treadmill guide</a>
            covers machines that pair with the widely supported Bluetooth FTMS standard.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty-and-trial" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. The 20-Year Warranty and the 30-Day Trial, Read Carefully</h2>
<p>
            Aviron's 20-year warranty is the longest we have seen on a consumer treadmill, and it is
            conditional. Garage Gym Reviews' Podium and Summit reviews both mark it down for being
            less generous than the headline suggests, and Aviron's own terms page explains why.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The two warranty tiers</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>20-year limited warranty.</strong> Covers the drive motor, incline motor, touchscreen, deck, belt, control pad and frame. It requires that you buy directly through Aviron's website, activate the membership within 90 days of ordering, and keep paying the initial monthly or annual membership. Aviron's terms say perpetual memberships do not qualify.</li>
            <li><strong>Standard limited warranty.</strong> Ten years on the frame and drive motor and one year on all other parts. This is what you have if you cancel the membership, buy a perpetual plan, or buy from anyone other than Aviron.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Three further details from Aviron's terms matter. The warranty is not transferable, so a
            used Aviron has none. On-site labour is excluded, as is commercial use. And there is a
            discrepancy to resolve before you rely on it: Aviron's product pages say the 20-year tier
            covers damage during moving, loss and theft, while its warranty terms list stolen units
            among the exclusions and say nothing about loss or moving damage. Ask Aviron which
            applies, and get the answer in writing.
          </p>
          <p>
            The retail point is easy to miss. Aviron's press release says the treadmills are expected
            at Best Buy, Dick's Sporting Goods and Amazon for the holiday season. Because the 20-year
            tier needs a direct purchase, a treadmill bought from a retailer would, on Aviron's
            current terms, carry the standard 10-year and one-year cover even with a membership. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a>
            explains how to compare these terms with other brands.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The 30-day trial</h3>
          <p>
            Aviron and Garage Gym Reviews both describe a 30-day risk-free trial. Aviron's policy says
            the request must be made within 30 days of delivery, Aviron supplies the return label, and
            there is no restocking fee. The conditions are worth reading, though: the treadmill must
            go back in its original box and packaging, in new condition with every part; the $299
            White Glove fee is not refunded; returns are not available to Alaska or Hawaii; and after
            the trial, the standard policy deducts return shipping and a 25% restocking fee. Keep
            the box for the first month. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-return-policies/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill return policy guide</a>
            compares these terms with other brands'.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="summit-incline" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. The Summit’s 42% Incline: What It Is, and Who It Is For</h2>
<p>
            A 42% grade means the deck rises 42 units for every 100 travelled — an angle of about
            23 degrees, far steeper than most roads and steeper than many hiking trails. Most home
            treadmills stop at 15%, and the steepest we had previously confirmed, in our guide to
            <a href="/treadmills-with-the-highest-incline/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills with the highest incline</a>,
            reach 40%. Aviron, Garage Gym Reviews and Wellworthy all give the Summit 42% with a -6%
            decline.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Speed falls as the grade rises</h3>
          <p>
            Aviron publishes a speed range for each band of grade. From -6% to 0% the belt runs 1 to
            12 mph; from 0% to 15%, 1 to 14 mph; from 15.5% to 25%, 1 to 8 mph; and from 25.5% to
            42%, 1 to 6 mph. In other words the Summit is a running treadmill on the flat and a
            walking and hiking machine on the steep grades; you cannot sprint up 30%. Aviron
            recommends a ceiling of at least 8 ft to use the full range, because the front of the
            deck rises a long way.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Is steep incline walking for you?</h3>
          <p>
            Walking uphill raises the effort considerably at a modest speed, which is why incline
            walking is popular with people who do not want to run. The CDC's guidelines for adults
            are at least 150 minutes of moderate-intensity activity a week, and incline walking is
            one way to reach it. Steep grades also load the calves, Achilles tendons and lower back
            differently from flat walking, so build up gradually, hold the handles for balance rather
            than for support, and stop if you feel pain. This site reviews equipment and does not
            give medical advice: if you have a heart, lung, joint or balance condition, or have been
            inactive for a long time, check with a clinician before using steep grades. Our
            <a href="/incline-trainer-vs-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">incline trainer vs treadmill guide</a>
            sets out who gains from more than 15%.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you will seldom go above 15 to 18%, the Podium gives you the same motor and screens,
            folds, and costs $500 less.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="recalls-and-safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Aviron Recalls and Treadmill Safety</h2>
<p>
            We searched the US Consumer Product Safety Commission's recall database on 20 October
            2026 for "Aviron" in recall titles, product names, manufacturer names and descriptions,
            and found no recall of any Aviron product — treadmill, rower or bike. A wider web search,
            including Health Canada's recall listings, also found none. That is a clean record, but a
            short one: Aviron's first treadmill only began delivering in 2025, and the current three
            have been shipping since September 2026.
          </p>
          <p>
            Recalls elsewhere in connected fitness show what to keep an eye on. Peloton's 2021 Tread+
            recall followed a child's death after being pulled under the rear of the machine, and a
            loose-screen recall of the standard Tread followed; our Peloton guide covers both. In
            2025 iFIT recalled NordicTrack rowing machines because the console could overheat. Large
            touchscreens mounted on tall uprights, and belts low to the floor, are the features to
            treat with care on any connected treadmill.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Habits that matter on any treadmill</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Children and pets.</strong> Keep them out of the room while the belt runs, and remove the safety key after every session so the treadmill cannot be started.</li>
            <li><strong>The safety clip.</strong> Attach it to your clothing every time, and especially on the Summit's steep grades, where a misstep is harder to recover from.</li>
            <li><strong>Clearance behind the deck.</strong> Aviron's pages we read did not state a rear clearance; use the ASTM treadmill standard's figures of at least 2 metres (about 6.5 feet) clear behind and 0.5 metres (about 20 inches) at each side.</li>
            <li><strong>Register the machine.</strong> Registering with Aviron is how you would hear about a future recall.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/treadmill-safety-tips/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill safety tips</a>
            go through set-up and everyday use in more detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="space-and-setup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Space, Weight, Assembly and the WalkDesk</h2>
<p>
            Two of the three fold, but none is small. Measure for the in-use size, the folded size
            if that is how it will spend most of its time, and the route into the room.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Floor space</h3>
          <p>
            The Justice and Podium are both 80 inches long in use, 34 and 37 inches wide
            respectively. Folded, Aviron gives the Justice 53 inches long and the Podium 49, both
            about 68 inches tall, which Aviron puts at roughly 12.5 square feet of floor. The Summit
            stays at 67 by 37 inches and 71 inches tall. Garage Gym Reviews notes that the folded
            Podium can be tipped and rolled on its wheels, and that the Summit has transport wheels
            but may not fit through some doorways and will not go easily up or down stairs.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Weight and floors</h3>
          <p>
            At 251, 284 and 364 lb before anyone stands on them, these are heavy machines. Put them on
            a ground floor or a solid, level floor, and use a firm mat to protect flooring. If the
            route in involves stairs, White Glove delivery at $299 is likely to be worth it,
            especially for the Summit.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Assembly</h3>
          <p>
            Aviron says the treadmills arrive about 80% assembled and take about an hour to finish
            with the included tools and video, and recommends two people. Garage Gym Reviews' Summit
            review reports an hour was accurate, with the uprights, console and screen to attach.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Noise and power</h3>
          <p>
            Aviron gives about 40 dB at walking speed and about 70 dB at top speed, figures we have
            not seen measured independently; footfall usually matters more than the motor on an
            upstairs floor. Aviron's pages we read did not state an electrical requirement. Ask
            whether a dedicated circuit is needed, and do not run any treadmill from an ordinary
            extension lead.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The WalkDesk</h3>
          <p>
            The WalkDesk is a $119 attachment for the Justice and Podium that Aviron says stays level
            when the deck is inclined, turning the treadmill into a walking workstation. It is a
            reasonable way to get a desk treadmill and a running treadmill in one, though a folding
            treadmill with a desk needs the same floor space as the treadmill alone, and a dedicated
            walking pad under an existing standing desk costs far less if you will never run.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Who Should Buy Something Else</h2>
<p>
            An Aviron treadmill suits a specific buyer: someone who is motivated by games and
            competition more than by instructors, and who will keep the membership. These are the
            cases where we would look elsewhere first.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>You want instructor-led classes.</strong> Aviron has coached classes, but its
            library is built around games. Peloton's Tread range and iFIT on NordicTrack and ProForm
            are built around instructors; iFIT also adjusts speed and incline along filmed routes. Our
            <a href="/best-smart-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">smart treadmill roundup</a>
            sets the platforms side by side.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>You are not sure you will keep paying.</strong> Aviron's free features are
            generous, but the 20-year warranty disappears with the membership. A treadmill whose
            console works fully on its own and whose warranty does not depend on a subscription
            keeps its value either way; our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to treadmills without subscriptions</a>
            lists them.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>You weigh more than 350 lb.</strong> All three current Avirons are outside their
            stated limits, and the Justice tops out at 300 lb. Several incline trainers and
            subscription-free treadmills are rated to 400 lb.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>You want steep incline without the games.</strong> NordicTrack's incline
            trainers reach 40% with a 6% decline and a 400 lb rating, on iFIT rather than Aviron. Our
            highest-incline guide compares them with the Summit's class of machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>You want to row as well.</strong> Aviron's rower is the product it is best known
            for, and one membership covers both machines for an extra $10 a month. Our
            <a href="/best-rowing-machines/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">rowing machine roundup</a>
            explains how connected rowers compare.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy the Aviron if</strong> the games are what will get you on the belt, you will
            keep a monthly or annual membership, you buy direct so the 20-year cover applies, and you
            fit the limits. In that case the Podium is the one to buy, the Justice if you mainly walk
            and the budget is tight, and the Summit only if you will really use the steep grades.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Aviron make a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. As of 20 October 2026 Aviron sells three treadmills in the US: the folding Justice ($1,999), the folding Podium ($2,499) and the non-folding Summit ($2,999), at Aviron’s sale prices with the 16-inch screen. Its earlier Victory treadmill is no longer listed on Aviron’s own store.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use an Aviron treadmill without a membership?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Aviron says the membership is not required. Without it you keep the metrics screen, manual speed and incline, profiles, workout history and streaming apps such as Netflix and YouTube. The games, classes, scenic routes and competitions need the membership, and so does the 20-year warranty.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much is the Aviron membership?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On 20 October 2026 Aviron charged $29 a month billed annually ($348 a year), $34 a month month to month, or $2,499 once for a perpetual plan. Each extra Aviron machine adds $10 a month, or $999 on a perpetual plan, and one membership covers unlimited household profiles.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Aviron 20-year warranty real?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is real but conditional. Aviron’s terms require a purchase direct from its website, membership activation within 90 days and continued payment of a monthly or annual plan; perpetual plans do not qualify. Otherwise the warranty is 10 years on the frame and drive motor and one year on other parts, and it does not transfer.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between the Aviron Justice, Podium and Summit?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Justice has a 3.5 CHP motor, 12.5 mph, 15% incline, a 20 by 59-inch deck and a 300 lb limit, and folds. The Podium has a 4.5 CHP motor, 14 mph, 18% incline, a 22 by 60-inch deck and a 350 lb limit, and also folds. The Summit adds a -6% to 42% grade range on a 22 by 61-inch deck, does not fold and weighs 364 lb.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Has the Aviron treadmill been recalled?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                We found no recall of any Aviron product when we searched the US Consumer Product Safety Commission’s recall database on 20 October 2026, and none in Health Canada’s listings. Register your treadmill with Aviron so you would be told of any future recall.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use the Aviron app on another treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not in any way we could confirm. Aviron’s standalone app launched in 2024 for Concept2 rowers, and we found no Aviron page listing a supported third-party treadmill. Some makers, including TKO, name Aviron as a compatible app, but confirm with Aviron before buying on that basis.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying an Aviron Treadmill</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most disappointing Aviron purchases go wrong on the terms rather than the machine. These are the four we would avoid.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming the 20-year warranty comes with the treadmill</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">It comes with a direct purchase and a recurring membership that you keep paying, activated within 90 days. Buy from a retailer, choose the perpetual plan or cancel, and Aviron’s terms give you 10 years on the frame and drive motor and one year on everything else. Decide which you are buying before you order.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the Summit for a room with a low ceiling</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Aviron recommends at least an 8 ft ceiling to use the Summit’s full incline range, because the front of the deck rises a long way at 42%. Measure the ceiling, add the height of the tallest user standing on a raised deck, and choose the Podium if the room cannot take it.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Throwing away the box in the first month</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Aviron’s 30-day trial requires the treadmill to go back in its original box and packaging, in new condition. After 30 days the standard policy deducts return shipping and a 25% restocking fee, and the $299 White Glove fee is never refunded. Keep the packaging until you are sure.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a used or third-party Victory expecting the warranty</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Aviron’s warranty does not transfer to a second owner and excludes units from unauthorised sellers. A Victory found on a marketplace may carry no Aviron cover at all. Ask the seller in writing what warranty applies and whose name it is in, and compare the price with a new Justice.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

