        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Aviron does sell treadmills in the US, and for someone who
          will keep the membership because the games get them moving, the $2,499 Podium is the one to
          buy.</strong> The $1,999 Justice suits walkers and joggers under 300 lb on a budget, and
          the $2,999 Summit is only worth its extra cost and weight if you will really use grades
          beyond 18%.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Buy direct and keep a monthly or annual plan if the 20-year warranty matters to you, keep the
          box for the 30-day trial, and get the theft and moving-damage cover confirmed in writing.
          If you are less sure about a subscription, a treadmill that works fully on its own is the
          safer buy — our
          <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">guide to treadmills without subscriptions</a>
          lists them.
          </p>
